ile the control group took 161 minutes—a 55.3% difference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradicting Evidence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Critics argue this was a &quot;greenfield&quot; task (starting from scratch) which favors AI.</li>\n<li>In complex, legacy enterprise systems where 70% of time is spent reading code rather than writing it, productivity gains are reported to be significantly lower (often cited between 10-20% by McKinsey and Gartner).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>✅ 2. The widespread use of AI is leading to a decrease in code quality and a signific</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>... (truncated for display, 95 characters omitted)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Verified</p>\n<p><strong>Source:</strong> GitClear (2024 White Paper: &quot;Coding on Copilot: 2023 Data and Observations&quot;)</p>\n<p><strong>Confidence:</strong> High</p>\n<p><strong>Supporting Evidence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An analysis of 153 million lines of code committed between 2020 and 2023 found that &quot;added code&quot; and &quot;churned code&quot; increased significantly in 2023.</li>\n<li>The study suggests AI encourages &quot;copy-pasting&quot; rather than refactoring, leading to higher technical debt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contradicting Evidence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proponents argue that AI can be used to improve quality through automated unit testing and linting, though this requires intentional human oversight.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>⏳ 3. Approximately 40% of AI-generated code contains security vulnerabilities.</h3>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Verified (Historical Context)</p>\n<p><strong>Source:</strong> NYU Tandon School of Engineering (2021 study: &quot;Asleep at the Keyboard? Assessing the Security of GitHub Copilot's Code Contributions&quot;)</p>\n<p><strong>Confidence:</strong> High</p>\n<p><strong>Supporting Evidence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Researchers prompted Copilot to generate code for 1,692 programs across 89 different scenarios.</li>\n<li>39.3